[s0l0!-header][h1]Input[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

C supports a number of ways for taking user input.
[b]getchar()[/b] Returns the value of the next single character input. 

[b][/b][s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1622"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  char a = [b]getchar()[/b];

  printf("You entered: %c", a);

  return 0;
}[/code]
The input is stored in the variable [b]a[/b].

The [b]gets()[/b] function is used to read input as an ordered sequence of characters, also called a [b]string[/b].
A string is stored in a char array.
[b][/b][s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1623"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  char a[100];

  [b]gets[/b](a); 

  printf("You entered: %s", a);

  return 0;
}[/code]
Here we stored the input in an array of 100 characters.

[b]scanf()[/b] scans input that matches format specifiers.

[b][/b][s0l0!-header][b]For example[/b]:[/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1624"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int a;
  [b]scanf[/b]("%d", &a);

  [b]printf[/b]("You entered: %d", a);

  return 0;
}[/code]
The [b]&[/b] sign before the variable name is the [b]address operator[/b]. It gives the address, or location in memory, of a variable. This is needed because [b]scanf [/b]places an input value at a variable address

As another example, let's prompt for two integer inputs and output their sum: [code format="c" codeId="1625"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int a, b;
  printf("Enter two numbers:");
  [b]scanf[/b]("%d %d", &a, &b);

  [b]printf[/b]("\nSum: %d", a+b);

  return 0;
}[/code]
[note][b][/b][b]scanf()[/b] stops reading as soon as it encounters a space, so text such as "Hello World" is two separate inputs for [b]scanf()[/b].[/note]

