[s0l0!-header][h1]Formatted Input[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]scanf[/b]() function is used to assign input to variables. A call to this function scans input according to format specifiers that convert input as necessary. 
If input can't be converted, then the assignment isn't made.
The [b]scanf[/b]() statement waits for input and then makes assignments: [code format="c" codeId="1752"]int x;
float num;
char text[20];
[b]scanf[/b]("%d %f %s", &x, &num, text); [/code]
Typing 10 22.5 abcd and then pressing Enter assigns 10 to x, 22.5 to num, and abcd to text. 
Note that the [b]&[/b] must be used to access the variable addresses. The [b]&[/b] isn't needed for a string because a string name acts as a pointer.

Format specifiers begin with a percent sign [b]%[/b] and are used to assign values to corresponding arguments after the control string. [b]Blanks, tabs, and newlines are ignored[/b]. 
A format specifier can include several options along with a conversion character:
[b]%[*][max_field]conversion character[/b]
The optional * will skip the input field.
The optional max_width gives the maximum number of characters to assign to an input field.
The conversion character converts the argument, if necessary, to the indicated type:
[b]d[/b] decimal
[b]c[/b] character
[b]s[/b] string
[b]f[/b] float
[b]x[/b] hexadecimal

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1753"]int x, y;
char text[20];

scanf("%2d %d %*f %5s", &x, &y, text);
/* input: 1234  5.7  elephant */
printf("%d  %d  %s", x, y, text);
/* output: 12  34  eleph */ [/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

