[s0l0!-header][h1]Formatting Output[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

The [b]printf [/b]function was introduced in your very first Hello World program. A call to this function requires a [b]format string[/b] which can include escape sequences for outputting special characters and format specifiers that are replaced by values.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1649"]printf("The tree has %d apples.\n", 22);
/* The tree has 22 apples. */

printf("\"Hello World!\"\n");
/* "Hello World!" */ [/code]
Escape sequences begin with a [b]backslash \[/b]:
[b]\n[/b] new line
[b]\t[/b] horizontal tab
[b]\\[/b] backslash
[b]\b[/b] backspace
[b]\'[/b] single quote
[b]\"[/b] double quote

Format specifiers begin with a[b] percent sign %[/b] and are replaced by corresponding arguments after the format string. A format specifier can include several options along with a conversion character: [code format="raw" ]%[-][width].[precision]conversion character [/code]
The optional [b]-[/b] specifies left alignment of the data in the string.
The optional [b]width [/b]gives the minimum number of characters for the data.
The [b]period .[/b] separates the [b]width [/b]from the [b]precision[/b].
The optional [b]precision [/b]gives the number of decimal places for numeric data. If [b]s[/b] is used as the conversion character, then precision determines the number of characters to print.
The conversion character converts the argument, if necessary, to the indicated type:
[b]d[/b] decimal
[b]c[/b] character
[b]s[/b] string
[b]f[/b] float
[b]e[/b] scientific notation
[b]x[/b] hexadecimal

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1650"]printf("Color: %s, Number: %d, float: %5.2f \n", "red", 42, 3.14159);
/* Color: red, Number: 42, float:  3.14  */

printf("Pi = %3.2f", 3.14159); 
/* Pi = 3.14 */

printf("Pi = %8.5f", 3.14159); 
/* Pi =   3.14159 */

printf("Pi = %-8.5f", 3.14159); 
/* Pi = 3.14159 */

printf("There are %d %s in the tree.", 22, "apples");
/* There are 22 apples in the tree. */ [/code]
To print the % symbol, use %% in the format string.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

